1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
21st century warfare.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:09,000
U.S. soldiers are often stationed behind enemy lines,

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
from mountain tops to barren deserts.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
But no matter where soldiers find themselves,

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
they need a base of operations.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
If you don't have protection, then everything else goes.

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:28,000
The equipment to keep them safe.

8
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:32,240
I wish I would have had something like this when I was deployed.

9
00:00:32,241 --> 00:00:34,000
And training that's as real as it gets.

10
00:00:34,001 --> 00:00:37,200
Great, Eddie, blow it up.

11
00:00:37,201 --> 00:00:43,600
Settle in as we find out what makes the military battle ready on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:59,980
Preparing a soldier for battle starts with training.

13
00:00:59,981 --> 00:01:03,460
For wars as recent as Vietnam, boot camp

14
00:01:03,461 --> 00:01:08,480
was the main form of preparation, obstacle courses,

15
00:01:08,481 --> 00:01:10,380
and calisthenics.

16
00:01:10,381 --> 00:01:13,740
but they did little to get troops ready for life in the jungle

17
00:01:13,741 --> 00:01:16,380
or how to cope with a foreign enemy in a foreign land.

18
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:26,340
Now, training is a full-scale, immersive experience.

19
00:01:26,341 --> 00:01:29,140
At Fort Irwin in California's Mojave Desert,

20
00:01:29,141 --> 00:01:33,580
the military runs simulated mission exercises six days a week,

21
00:01:33,581 --> 00:01:37,540
with about 50,000 troops coming through each year.

22
00:01:37,541 --> 00:01:41,980
This is a rare glimpse into one of the National Training Center's war game sessions.

23
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,860
Keep your heads on a swivel. Make sure you're scanning your lanes.

24
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:51,720
The goal is to give soldiers a realistic experience to prepare them for deployment overseas.

25
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:58,880
In Afghanistan, game planning a convoy through unknown territory is a life-or-death prospect.

26
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:02,300
Ambushes can happen at any time.

27
00:02:02,301 --> 00:02:05,140
Come up, come up, come up, come up, come up, come up.

28
00:02:05,141 --> 00:02:08,980
Anyone you pass can be an ally or an enemy.

29
00:02:08,981 --> 00:02:10,400
Soldiers can never be sure.

30
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:14,020
Four, fire four.

31
00:02:14,021 --> 00:02:15,940
When a battle does break out,

32
00:02:15,941 --> 00:02:19,020
instinct needs to drive a soldier, not panic.

33
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:27,860
Today, there were massive casualties

34
00:02:27,861 --> 00:02:31,600
and several missteps throughout the battle.

35
00:02:31,601 --> 00:02:33,400
Hey, good job, man.

36
00:02:33,401 --> 00:02:34,900
And nice death, too.

37
00:02:34,901 --> 00:02:38,000
That was pretty good.

38
00:02:38,001 --> 00:02:42,240
Luckily, it was just a training exercise.

39
00:02:42,241 --> 00:02:45,220
On a base the size of Rhode Island,

40
00:02:45,221 --> 00:02:48,320
the army has recreated two Middle Eastern villages

41
00:02:48,321 --> 00:02:51,660
with more than 1,000 building facades,

42
00:02:51,661 --> 00:02:53,520
even a functioning mosque.

43
00:02:53,521 --> 00:02:57,420
This village, it is exactly like Iraqi village.

44
00:02:57,421 --> 00:03:01,100
So the people, they act the same way.

45
00:03:01,101 --> 00:03:03,300
You know, they even had the blood and the scenario

46
00:03:03,301 --> 00:03:04,140
and things like that.

47
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:05,660
I mean, it was pretty realistic.

48
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:08,480
This is the way a training session plays out.

49
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:10,900
First, soldiers are given a mission.

50
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:14,400
Mission was to go into this village

51
00:03:14,401 --> 00:03:17,100
and link up with the police chief

52
00:03:17,101 --> 00:03:19,580
and then support their patrol.

53
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:23,920
Then, instructors like Sergeant First Class DiGaitano,

54
00:03:23,921 --> 00:03:26,000
a veteran of three tours in Iraq.

55
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:27,400
Hey, big boom, big boom.

56
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:29,900
Make sure everything goes wrong.

57
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:36,100
Once the training unit comes in and they get within sight distance,

58
00:03:36,101 --> 00:03:38,100
I ignite the initial explosion.

59
00:03:38,101 --> 00:03:40,100
Hit the cafe, hit the cafe. Bam.

60
00:03:40,101 --> 00:03:42,100
And then that's when the scenario begins.

61
00:03:42,101 --> 00:03:45,100
And then everything else I do is dependent upon how the unit is reacting.

62
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:50,100
The goal is to make soldiers stronger mentally

63
00:03:50,101 --> 00:03:53,100
by exposing them to the realities of war.

64
00:03:53,101 --> 00:03:56,100
We throw a lot at them in this scenario.

65
00:03:56,101 --> 00:04:00,100
It's basically created to be the worst possible patrol imaginable

66
00:04:00,101 --> 00:04:03,940
to stress their systems and to kind of expose their weaknesses.

67
00:04:03,941 --> 00:04:06,500
Well, it didn't go to plan.

68
00:04:06,501 --> 00:04:11,620
Pretty much everything went to, well, went the wrong way.

69
00:04:11,621 --> 00:04:12,620
Ready?

70
00:04:12,621 --> 00:04:13,940
Watch that window right there.

71
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:21,260
As chaos ensues, high-tech gear is at play.

72
00:04:21,261 --> 00:04:24,060
Everyone's actions are tracked with a multiple integrated

73
00:04:24,061 --> 00:04:27,100
laser system, or MILES.

74
00:04:27,101 --> 00:04:32,600
Like a laser tag vest, it beeps any time a soldier is shot.

75
00:04:32,601 --> 00:04:33,700
Hey, see, she's going off.

76
00:04:33,701 --> 00:04:35,500
Miles on Miles.

77
00:04:35,501 --> 00:04:36,900
No, she's alive.

78
00:04:36,901 --> 00:04:40,400
If one of my shooters up in the building fires upon them

79
00:04:40,401 --> 00:04:42,700
and the Miles goes off, then we adjudicate

80
00:04:42,701 --> 00:04:44,900
with a Miles casualty card.

81
00:04:44,901 --> 00:04:47,000
Ah, you're dead.

82
00:04:47,001 --> 00:04:49,000
A signal alerts both the participant

83
00:04:49,001 --> 00:04:51,700
and the officer in charge.

84
00:04:51,701 --> 00:04:54,000
At this point, the soldier is considered wounded

85
00:04:54,001 --> 00:04:57,000
and given a casualty card.

86
00:04:57,001 --> 00:04:58,260
And you can talk, but you can't walk.

87
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:00,940
All right.

88
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:03,300
Now, this one right here is a gunshot wound to the knee.

89
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:06,240
And it reads, casually needs treatment within 10 minutes,

90
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:07,240
or he becomes a KIA.

91
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:15,160
Tracking every movement are seven strategically placed

92
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:17,560
cameras lining the village.

93
00:05:17,660 --> 00:05:20,360
What we have here, we have cameras all set up all around

94
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:21,860
the lane that link up to the booth.

95
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:24,700
So they will have video snippets of what they did correct

96
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,000
and what they did wrong there so they can set up

97
00:05:27,001 --> 00:05:27,800
well, that didn't happen.

98
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:29,260
Well, video doesn't lie.

99
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:30,940
When the mission is complete,

100
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:33,400
and while it's still fresh in soldiers' minds,

101
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:35,300
they're pulled in for an assessment.

102
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,500
They'll do an after-action review.

103
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:40,240
There are combat trainers that are embedded with them.

104
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:42,780
They will let them know what went well.

105
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:45,440
They will also let them know what went wrong.

106
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:48,420
Major Thiessen runs through the assessments on film.

107
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:51,020
The crowd's basically just harassing them,

108
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:52,040
running them up.

109
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:54,200
Now, had they had a better partnership

110
00:05:54,201 --> 00:05:55,760
with their Afghan uniformed police,

111
00:05:55,761 --> 00:05:59,160
we would have affected the crowd, not harass them as much.

112
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:01,560
All the cameras are fed into this command center

113
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:05,260
and a state-of-the-art control board system.

114
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:07,740
All the technology that we put out here

115
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:09,880
drives the after-action review.

116
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:18,420
Trainees also face the number one threat in Afghanistan,

117
00:06:18,520 --> 00:06:22,960
improvised explosive devices, or IEDs.

118
00:06:22,961 --> 00:06:24,200
You accept .

119
00:06:24,201 --> 00:06:25,160
You watch your copies.

120
00:06:25,161 --> 00:06:27,660
They're happy first.

121
00:06:27,661 --> 00:06:29,860
Easily disguised, these bombs are made out

122
00:06:29,861 --> 00:06:32,400
of leftover artillery shells, scrap metal,

123
00:06:32,401 --> 00:06:35,200
and common household wires.

124
00:06:35,201 --> 00:06:38,300
You know, over in the theater, they will use anything they can

125
00:06:38,301 --> 00:06:39,920
to try to deceive the soldiers.

126
00:06:42,620 --> 00:06:43,820
And that's kind of what we do out here.

127
00:06:43,821 --> 00:06:44,720
We use barrels.

128
00:06:44,721 --> 00:06:45,820
We use cars.

129
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:52,320
So who does the military turn to

130
00:06:52,321 --> 00:06:54,380
for lifelike war zone pyrotechnics?

131
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:56,120
Hollywood, of course.

132
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:00,240
Stand by for an explosion.

133
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:08,380
This whole town is hardwired here into my firing tower.

134
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:11,400
I can see every IED that's implanted,

135
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:12,520
every bomb that's in place,

136
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:14,740
so I'm able to determine whether or not

137
00:07:14,741 --> 00:07:17,400
it's a safety issue before we detonate a bomb.

138
00:07:18,140 --> 00:07:20,400
Steve's pyro partner is Ed Pratt.

139
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:21,920
Throughout the whole town,

140
00:07:21,921 --> 00:07:24,260
We probably have about 30 devices.

141
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:26,820
They do their best to replicate

142
00:07:26,821 --> 00:07:29,220
what the soldiers will face in Afghanistan.

143
00:07:29,221 --> 00:07:30,260
This is the initiator.

144
00:07:30,261 --> 00:07:32,660
This is the one that actually makes a big, loud noise.

145
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:38,780
It shakes you up. I mean, it's a loud explosion.

146
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:43,540
They even have fake rubble.

147
00:07:43,541 --> 00:07:46,680
This is Hollywood cork, and what we call pyrolyte.

148
00:07:46,681 --> 00:07:48,780
It's dirt that gets thrown in the air,

149
00:07:48,781 --> 00:07:50,580
and it looks like rocks, but it doesn't hurt you.

150
00:07:51,920 --> 00:08:03,400
Nothing can truly prepare a soldier to cope with casualties,

151
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:08,340
but these exercises expose soldiers to the carnage of war

152
00:08:08,341 --> 00:08:09,500
they're likely to face.

153
00:08:10,620 --> 00:08:13,140
A lot of this unit right now is a lot of privates

154
00:08:13,141 --> 00:08:16,300
that have never deployed, so it's really kind of a little bit

155
00:08:16,301 --> 00:08:17,320
of a desensitization.

156
00:08:18,460 --> 00:08:21,040
Like, hey, this is what you might deal with when we're overseas.

157
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:25,160
Professional special effects can lessen the shock of bloodshed

158
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:27,220
in the field.

159
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:30,120
The Army employs up to 600 role players

160
00:08:30,220 --> 00:08:33,460
and about a dozen experts in Hollywood crafts.

161
00:08:33,560 --> 00:08:35,860
I'm a trained effects makeup artist.

162
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:37,960
I have a license for it.

163
00:08:38,060 --> 00:08:41,180
Didn't know this job existed, really.

164
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:42,580
It's pretty fun.

165
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:45,380
Here's a real bone, cow bone.

166
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:47,820
This is an eyeball that's popped out

167
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:49,420
because of the hair.

168
00:08:49,421 --> 00:08:53,260
the eyeball that's popped out because of a blunt force to the head.

169
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:57,560
Sergeant White is a veteran of both Iraq and Kosovo.

170
00:08:58,420 --> 00:09:00,720
Today, he's being dressed for a traumatic brain injury.

171
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:03,360
If I would have come here back in 2005

172
00:09:03,361 --> 00:09:06,100
and we would have had the makeup artist and all the other stuff,

173
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:10,240
I think once I did deploy and seen some real-life casualties,

174
00:09:10,860 --> 00:09:12,420
it would have made it a whole lot easier to deal with.

175
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:14,680
We've had a few people vomiting.

176
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:16,560
I hear about it.

177
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,820
I feel bad, but it makes me feel good.

178
00:09:18,821 --> 00:09:21,820
I did my job.

179
00:09:21,821 --> 00:09:23,700
The military tries to make the role players

180
00:09:23,701 --> 00:09:25,460
as real as possible.

181
00:09:25,461 --> 00:09:27,420
I'm actually a contractor,

182
00:09:27,421 --> 00:09:29,200
and we get hired here as amputees

183
00:09:29,201 --> 00:09:30,700
to portray wounded soldiers.

184
00:09:30,701 --> 00:09:32,000
It doesn't hurt a bit.

185
00:09:32,001 --> 00:09:33,700
Not a bit.

186
00:09:33,701 --> 00:09:35,160
Since I am an amputee,

187
00:09:35,161 --> 00:09:37,640
acting like an amputee is really easy.

188
00:09:37,641 --> 00:09:41,000
It helps prepare you for the actual real thing.

189
00:09:41,001 --> 00:09:43,520
I think it's about as close as you're gonna get.

190
00:09:45,620 --> 00:09:47,340
Sergeant First Class DiGaitano

191
00:09:47,341 --> 00:09:50,040
this new kind of training can make a huge difference

192
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:51,580
to soldiers.

193
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:53,380
So hopefully these soldiers that came through today

194
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:55,780
learned something that won't happen over there,

195
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:57,080
because I want them all to come back.

196
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:07,700
Once soldiers finish training and are ready to deploy,

197
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:09,800
the next step in being ready for battle

198
00:10:09,900 --> 00:10:14,140
is having a base for operations and protection.

199
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:16,440
Historically, a unit's base camp was a place

200
00:10:16,441 --> 00:10:21,280
a place of relative safety, well away from the battlefield.

201
00:10:21,281 --> 00:10:25,980
Now conflict can occur anywhere, at any time.

202
00:10:25,981 --> 00:10:29,200
We no longer have that forward line of troops.

203
00:10:29,201 --> 00:10:31,800
The enemy is all around us, and we have

204
00:10:31,801 --> 00:10:35,280
to set up little isolated areas of security.

205
00:10:35,281 --> 00:10:38,080
And from those little areas, we can project outwards.

206
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:46,620
Case in point, Kabul, Afghanistan, 2003.

207
00:10:46,621 --> 00:10:48,980
While exposed to enemy combatants,

208
00:10:48,981 --> 00:10:51,740
the army transformed this barren swath of desert

209
00:10:51,741 --> 00:10:58,320
just outside the city into what is now Camp Phoenix.

210
00:10:58,321 --> 00:11:02,620
This forward operating base stretches over 40 acres.

211
00:11:02,621 --> 00:11:07,380
It's able to accommodate 500-plus military personnel

212
00:11:07,381 --> 00:11:12,340
and train 17,000 Afghan National Army soldiers.

213
00:11:12,341 --> 00:11:16,240
This land went from empty to a fortified, fully operational

214
00:11:16,241 --> 00:11:24,280
base in 14 days, a remarkable feat for 2003.

215
00:11:24,281 --> 00:11:27,120
But today, these forward operating bases

216
00:11:27,121 --> 00:11:30,840
are lighter, faster, and stronger.

217
00:11:30,841 --> 00:11:34,100
At Fort Devens in Massachusetts, the military

218
00:11:34,101 --> 00:11:38,100
is refining and testing this faster technology,

219
00:11:38,101 --> 00:11:44,000
a 150-man base that sets up in just four hours.

220
00:11:44,001 --> 00:11:45,920
Those first days are absolutely precious

221
00:11:45,921 --> 00:11:47,420
in terms of military mission.

222
00:11:47,421 --> 00:11:50,700
What we are targeting is reducing the amount of time

223
00:11:50,701 --> 00:11:54,080
it takes to establish a base.

224
00:11:54,081 --> 00:11:59,160
By setting up faster, the Army is also saving lives.

225
00:11:59,161 --> 00:12:03,980
In those first few hours, soldiers are vulnerable to attack.

226
00:12:03,981 --> 00:12:07,740
This new and improved deployable base system is called

227
00:12:07,741 --> 00:12:09,300
Force Provider.

228
00:12:09,301 --> 00:12:12,140
Force Provider contains all the basic life support

229
00:12:12,141 --> 00:12:16,740
equipment that you need to sustain an operating base.

230
00:12:16,741 --> 00:12:17,900
Basically, a city in a can.

231
00:12:20,940 --> 00:12:25,160
It's hard to imagine, but an entire 150-person base camp

232
00:12:25,161 --> 00:12:28,760
can fit inside one C-17 aircraft.

233
00:12:28,761 --> 00:12:31,080
Within the plane are 15 containers,

234
00:12:31,081 --> 00:12:33,120
expertly packed with the necessary equipment.

235
00:12:34,940 --> 00:12:39,460
The command center, shelters, weapons, food, and showers

236
00:12:39,461 --> 00:12:40,780
are all included.

237
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:44,820
It's a base in a box.

238
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:50,560
You can get 150 people worth of habitation capability

239
00:12:50,561 --> 00:12:54,140
on one C-17 aircraft, which is pretty significant.

240
00:13:01,080 --> 00:13:06,080
All the contents on the plane start here at Fort Devens.

241
00:13:06,081 --> 00:13:12,080
When it opened, modern marvels got one of the first looks inside.

242
00:13:12,081 --> 00:13:16,080
This 10-acre facility is called the Systems Integration Lab,

243
00:13:16,081 --> 00:13:18,080
or SIL.

244
00:13:18,081 --> 00:13:20,080
Its mission is to research and refine the capabilities

245
00:13:20,081 --> 00:13:23,080
of the force provider base camps.

246
00:13:23,081 --> 00:13:25,080
It's an environment where we can test and compare

247
00:13:25,081 --> 00:13:28,080
and evaluate new technologies to see if it improves

248
00:13:28,081 --> 00:13:30,240
It improves the current systems that we have.

249
00:13:30,840 --> 00:13:33,300
The first force provider base camp design

250
00:13:33,301 --> 00:13:35,720
took 20 men over a week to set up.

251
00:13:36,580 --> 00:13:40,260
After 20 years of research, it's down to four hours.

252
00:13:41,380 --> 00:13:43,760
The base can be set up anywhere in the world

253
00:13:43,761 --> 00:13:46,940
in temperatures from 15 degrees below zero

254
00:13:46,941 --> 00:13:49,520
to 120 degrees above.

255
00:13:50,440 --> 00:13:51,500
Versatility is key.

256
00:13:52,140 --> 00:13:54,560
It's all about picking up and being able to move it

257
00:13:54,561 --> 00:13:56,020
in a very short period of time.

258
00:13:58,080 --> 00:14:06,620
The Force Provider camps all start with a basic sturdy building block, the Tricon shipping

259
00:14:06,621 --> 00:14:07,620
container.

260
00:14:07,621 --> 00:14:12,140
In order to be able to push Force Provider and to maintain that transportability, to

261
00:14:12,141 --> 00:14:17,680
maintain that deployability, the Tricon container is absolutely essential.

262
00:14:17,681 --> 00:14:22,320
Its standardized specs are 8 by 8 by 6 and a half.

263
00:14:22,321 --> 00:14:27,780
So all the equipment manufactured to pack into the box has the same size requirements.

264
00:14:27,781 --> 00:14:32,180
The Tricon is made out of steel, and it's actually made to the international shipping standard.

265
00:14:32,460 --> 00:14:36,700
So you can actually stack these Tricons in a shipping configuration nine high.

266
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:40,620
The Tricons can carry up to 10,000 pounds each.

267
00:14:41,540 --> 00:14:43,200
They can even be repurposed.

268
00:14:44,320 --> 00:14:51,260
This idea, still in development, is an eight-person bunking system that pops out of one Tricon container.

269
00:14:52,340 --> 00:14:56,020
This is like taking the next step of a shipping container and saying,

270
00:14:56,021 --> 00:14:57,560
And what else can we do with this?

271
00:14:57,561 --> 00:15:01,580
What else can we do to maintain that same footprint, maintain that transportability,

272
00:15:01,581 --> 00:15:03,840
that deployability?

273
00:15:03,841 --> 00:15:09,000
The walls are made of a fabric skin that enables them to slide out faster.

274
00:15:09,001 --> 00:15:14,120
The fabric then acts as insulation, controlling the room's air-conditioned environment, keeping

275
00:15:14,121 --> 00:15:18,240
the 120-degree desert air out.

276
00:15:18,241 --> 00:15:22,520
And it's just a future possible building solution that we're looking at a force provider to

277
00:15:22,521 --> 00:15:26,220
push out and give soldiers a better place to live.

278
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:33,040
Not surprisingly, many of the Army's innovations

279
00:15:33,041 --> 00:15:34,800
result from field experiences.

280
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:39,420
Good and bad, a deadly terrorist attack

281
00:15:39,421 --> 00:15:41,860
quickly exposes the need for improved protection.

282
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:48,980
1996, the bombing of the Khobar Towers in Saudi Arabia.

283
00:15:49,840 --> 00:15:52,300
A U.S. military task force has found

284
00:15:52,301 --> 00:15:54,860
that lacked security opened the way

285
00:15:54,861 --> 00:15:57,600
for the truck bombing of a U.S. Air Force complex

286
00:15:57,601 --> 00:15:58,800
in Saudi Arabia.

287
00:15:59,640 --> 00:16:01,680
This eight-story building housed

288
00:16:01,681 --> 00:16:03,320
United States Air Force personnel.

289
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:08,520
On June 25th, terrorists drove a truck bomb

290
00:16:08,521 --> 00:16:09,320
next to the building.

291
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:14,160
Only a chain-link fence and a couple of concrete barriers

292
00:16:14,161 --> 00:16:17,060
separated the parking lot from the housing compound.

293
00:16:19,540 --> 00:16:20,980
When the bomb exploded,

294
00:16:20,981 --> 00:16:25,520
19 US servicemen were dead, and more than 370 wounded.

295
00:16:29,220 --> 00:16:31,420
Had there been a more protective barrier,

296
00:16:31,421 --> 00:16:34,320
it's highly likely that the structural damage and number

297
00:16:34,321 --> 00:16:36,320
of casualties would have been reduced.

298
00:16:39,840 --> 00:16:41,560
This lesson underscored the importance

299
00:16:41,561 --> 00:16:44,840
of making a stronger fortress, especially when terrorism

300
00:16:44,841 --> 00:16:47,700
is a constant threat.

301
00:16:47,701 --> 00:16:50,980
As recently as the Vietnam War, big cities

302
00:16:50,981 --> 00:16:54,580
Base camps relied primarily on sandbag walls.

303
00:16:54,581 --> 00:16:56,680
But making a wall out of sandbags

304
00:16:56,681 --> 00:16:59,740
is labor-intensive and time-consuming.

305
00:16:59,741 --> 00:17:04,620
One person can only fill about 20 sandbags per hour.

306
00:17:04,621 --> 00:17:07,040
The military needed a faster and stronger form

307
00:17:07,041 --> 00:17:09,460
of perimeter protection.

308
00:17:09,461 --> 00:17:11,500
During the first Gulf War, the Army

309
00:17:11,501 --> 00:17:16,380
adopted a new perimeter system, the Hesco Concertainer Unit.

310
00:17:16,381 --> 00:17:18,880
Considered the most significant field fortification

311
00:17:18,881 --> 00:17:23,880
since World War II, it increased soldier safety dramatically.

312
00:17:23,881 --> 00:17:27,680
This particular barrier is collapsible,

313
00:17:27,681 --> 00:17:31,120
it's rapidly deployable, and it can go very quickly

314
00:17:31,121 --> 00:17:35,120
because it's light. The compact walls pack flat

315
00:17:35,121 --> 00:17:38,840
and can weigh over 2,000 pounds, depending on the length

316
00:17:38,841 --> 00:17:44,640
of the perimeter. One, two, three.

317
00:17:44,641 --> 00:17:48,640
The objective is to unfold the units, then fill them with dirt

318
00:17:48,641 --> 00:17:50,140
Simple, but very effective.

319
00:17:53,640 --> 00:17:56,980
Made of welded mesh steel and lined with a specially designed

320
00:17:57,080 --> 00:18:01,580
geotextile, these walls are adaptable.

321
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:03,480
There's an old adage in our community that is,

322
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:05,600
if you've seen one base, you've seen one base.

323
00:18:05,700 --> 00:18:09,400
They come in all shapes, sizes, varieties.

324
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:12,400
They actually latch together, and they're interlocked

325
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:15,500
so that it's harder to break through them.

326
00:18:15,501 --> 00:18:19,900
You can fill these up with sand or dirt that is readily available,

327
00:18:19,901 --> 00:18:25,880
and it becomes a barrier between you and the threat that may be on the other side of it.

328
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:35,060
The HESCO units are also tough enough to stop a 7.5-ton truck traveling 50 miles per hour,

329
00:18:36,540 --> 00:18:37,680
dead in its tracks.

330
00:18:38,460 --> 00:18:42,580
In addition, the walls can sustain explosive charges up to 20,000 pounds.

331
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:52,500
A typical HESCO defense wall is equivalent to 1,500 sandbags.

332
00:18:52,501 --> 00:18:58,500
The sandbag wall would take 10 men about seven hours to build.

333
00:18:58,501 --> 00:19:02,500
The HESCO wall, from stacked to packed,

334
00:19:02,501 --> 00:19:07,500
it takes two men and a common front loader 20 minutes.

335
00:19:07,501 --> 00:19:10,500
It seems very simple, but it's one of those cutting-edge things

336
00:19:10,501 --> 00:19:14,500
that really has provided the force protection, saved lives.

337
00:19:14,501 --> 00:19:16,000
saved lives in Iraq.

338
00:19:23,300 --> 00:19:26,180
Today's soldier faces an enemy unlike most others

339
00:19:26,181 --> 00:19:30,240
in history, less like World War II and more

340
00:19:30,241 --> 00:19:34,080
like guerrilla warfare.

341
00:19:34,081 --> 00:19:36,620
In Afghanistan, the enemy makes itself known

342
00:19:36,621 --> 00:19:42,000
by blindsiding soldiers with hidden landmines,

343
00:19:42,001 --> 00:19:45,240
Improvised Explosive Devices, or IEDs.

344
00:19:48,840 --> 00:19:52,020
When the war in Afghanistan began in 2001,

345
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:55,060
fatalities from IEDs were relatively low.

346
00:19:56,400 --> 00:19:58,080
But as the war continued,

347
00:19:58,081 --> 00:20:01,200
the frequency of IED encounters steadily increased.

348
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:08,260
In 2010 alone,

349
00:20:08,261 --> 00:20:13,260
There were 368 IED-related deaths in Afghanistan.

350
00:20:13,360 --> 00:20:18,260
It's now the chief cause of U.S. casualties there.

351
00:20:18,360 --> 00:20:22,200
Up until a few years ago, we had no preventative measure

352
00:20:22,300 --> 00:20:26,220
to, you know, protect them.

353
00:20:26,320 --> 00:20:29,220
With a new threat comes new methods of protection

354
00:20:29,320 --> 00:20:32,620
to ensure soldiers are battle-ready.

355
00:20:32,720 --> 00:20:35,820
You may not think of it at first, but a soldier's safety

356
00:20:35,821 --> 00:20:39,080
starts with what he's wearing, the standard issue cotton

357
00:20:39,160 --> 00:20:40,360
uniform.

358
00:20:40,460 --> 00:20:43,560
And now it's getting an overhaul.

359
00:20:43,660 --> 00:20:46,300
The Natick Research Lab in Massachusetts

360
00:20:46,400 --> 00:20:49,100
is testing an improved fire retardant uniform.

361
00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:53,980
We perform the Army's thermal testing on the fabrics.

362
00:20:54,080 --> 00:20:56,900
There's actually a lot of different areas of research

363
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:00,840
that go into creating the uniforms, all the way from the

364
00:21:00,940 --> 00:21:02,840
dyes to color them, to the threads,

365
00:21:02,940 --> 00:21:05,280
to how things are stitched together to give the soldier

366
00:21:05,281 --> 00:21:08,040
the best optimized uniform.

367
00:21:08,140 --> 00:21:10,380
Nedick has been working on developing safer,

368
00:21:10,480 --> 00:21:14,620
more advanced uniform fabrics to help limit burn injuries.

369
00:21:14,720 --> 00:21:17,620
There's a trade-off between wearability, usability,

370
00:21:17,720 --> 00:21:18,920
and flame resistance.

371
00:21:19,020 --> 00:21:21,740
We can make someone totally flame resistant,

372
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:24,140
but they would die of heat stroke.

373
00:21:24,240 --> 00:21:26,740
So to make a combat uniform both flame resistant

374
00:21:26,840 --> 00:21:30,180
and breathable, they weave a synthetic fiber similar to Kevlar

375
00:21:30,280 --> 00:21:32,640
into the fabric.

376
00:21:32,641 --> 00:21:37,860
it's stronger than steel and typically used in bulletproof vests.

377
00:21:40,360 --> 00:21:43,800
The fabric runs between $16 and $21 a yard,

378
00:21:44,300 --> 00:21:47,120
so it's more expensive than cotton, but worth it.

379
00:21:47,540 --> 00:21:52,000
The cost to develop a full-scale uniform is quite expensive.

380
00:21:52,140 --> 00:21:56,060
You actually get the patterns, the different layout, the assembly of them.

381
00:21:56,061 --> 00:21:58,840
So it costs hundreds and hundreds of dollars.

382
00:21:58,841 --> 00:22:01,340
In the first stage of testing,

383
00:22:01,341 --> 00:22:04,840
the fabric must withstand the heat of an IED blast.

384
00:22:04,841 --> 00:22:07,840
They use lasers to mimic the initial explosion.

385
00:22:07,841 --> 00:22:10,840
This is a 2,500-watt CO2 laser

386
00:22:10,841 --> 00:22:13,840
used in industry to cut steel plates.

387
00:22:13,841 --> 00:22:16,840
It is the quickest, least expensive way

388
00:22:16,841 --> 00:22:21,840
we could figure out to simulate an IED thermal signature.

389
00:22:21,841 --> 00:22:24,840
The laser pop happens quickly.

390
00:22:24,841 --> 00:22:26,840
Ready?

391
00:22:26,841 --> 00:22:30,840
If you blink, you'll miss it.

392
00:22:30,841 --> 00:22:32,840
Real fast and sustained burst,

393
00:22:32,841 --> 00:22:36,840
it simulates the thermal blast of an IED event.

394
00:22:36,841 --> 00:22:39,840
It's safe.

395
00:22:39,841 --> 00:22:42,840
This flame-resistant technology is only three years old.

396
00:22:42,841 --> 00:22:47,840
The urgency for it increased as the threat of IEDs escalated.

397
00:22:47,841 --> 00:22:48,840
Wonderful.

398
00:22:48,841 --> 00:22:52,840
You can see from the back, there is no burn through.

399
00:22:52,841 --> 00:22:55,840
So there's still fabric protecting him.

400
00:22:55,841 --> 00:22:59,180
After the initial blast, the soldier

401
00:22:59,181 --> 00:23:01,840
may face prolonged exposure to flames.

402
00:23:01,841 --> 00:23:05,180
This next demo tests the uniform's fire resistance.

403
00:23:05,181 --> 00:23:08,080
This simulates the aftermath of an IED.

404
00:23:08,081 --> 00:23:11,080
Something is burning, and they're in it.

405
00:23:11,081 --> 00:23:13,600
Natick uses mannequins loaded with heat sensors

406
00:23:13,601 --> 00:23:15,500
to collect the data.

407
00:23:15,501 --> 00:23:17,500
It has 123 sensors.

408
00:23:17,501 --> 00:23:20,400
He's articulated, so he actually will move,

409
00:23:20,401 --> 00:23:22,900
which is very important when you're testing a uniform.

410
00:23:22,901 --> 00:23:26,900
When you're testing a uniform, we can assess whether they have a first, second, or third

411
00:23:26,901 --> 00:23:30,780
degree burn, what kind of protection the uniform is actually providing.

412
00:23:30,781 --> 00:23:34,420
For this test, they bring out the big guns.

413
00:23:34,421 --> 00:23:39,860
We have eight torches, bazookas, shooting raw propane.

414
00:23:39,861 --> 00:23:45,120
Uniform fabrics have come a long way since the simple cotton ones of the 1940s.

415
00:23:45,121 --> 00:23:49,900
Today uniforms are as light and as strong as technology can make them.

416
00:23:49,901 --> 00:23:51,520
Energized, flame on.

417
00:23:52,900 --> 00:23:57,900
It may be just fabric, but it can save lives.

418
00:23:57,901 --> 00:23:59,900
You get a cotton uniform, it starts flaming up.

419
00:23:59,901 --> 00:24:00,900
It doesn't self-extinguish.

420
00:24:00,901 --> 00:24:01,900
This self-extinguishes.

421
00:24:12,900 --> 00:24:13,900
How'd we do?

422
00:24:13,901 --> 00:24:14,900
We did pretty well.

423
00:24:14,901 --> 00:24:16,900
They were pretty well protected.

424
00:24:16,901 --> 00:24:19,900
The uniform did not ignite.

425
00:24:19,901 --> 00:24:21,900
It passed the test.

426
00:24:21,901 --> 00:24:24,740
I don't think people realize all the different technologies

427
00:24:24,741 --> 00:24:27,840
that go into the uniform that actually protect the soldier.

428
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:32,420
It's another example of making the soldier more battle-ready.

429
00:24:34,020 --> 00:24:35,400
Forward, march.

430
00:24:38,740 --> 00:24:40,440
But when it comes to protection,

431
00:24:40,880 --> 00:24:43,020
just as important as a soldier's uniform

432
00:24:43,021 --> 00:24:45,340
is the place he rests his head at night.

433
00:24:46,120 --> 00:24:48,280
The force provider Base in a Box

434
00:24:48,281 --> 00:24:50,600
has introduced major changes to the base camp,

435
00:24:50,601 --> 00:24:55,540
But the tent is still the standard form of shelter.

436
00:24:55,640 --> 00:24:57,640
Since the beginning of mobile armies,

437
00:24:57,740 --> 00:25:00,940
soldiers have used tents for housing.

438
00:25:01,040 --> 00:25:02,940
The Romans' tents were made of leather

439
00:25:03,040 --> 00:25:05,140
and slept up to eight people.

440
00:25:05,240 --> 00:25:08,160
But they had little ventilation and were labor intensive

441
00:25:08,260 --> 00:25:10,060
to set up.

442
00:25:10,160 --> 00:25:12,660
For most people, time is money.

443
00:25:12,760 --> 00:25:17,460
But for the military, time equals lives.

444
00:25:17,560 --> 00:25:19,660
Even when canvas tents came along,

445
00:25:19,661 --> 00:25:22,420
Setting them up was never a quick process,

446
00:25:22,520 --> 00:25:25,660
leaving soldiers vulnerable to attack.

447
00:25:25,760 --> 00:25:29,400
Improving setup became mission critical.

448
00:25:29,500 --> 00:25:32,800
Our focus is enabling the mission,

449
00:25:32,900 --> 00:25:37,420
enabling the soldier, and reducing the risk and vulnerabilities.

450
00:25:37,520 --> 00:25:41,620
World War II canvas tents required long, bulky wooden poles

451
00:25:41,720 --> 00:25:44,120
that were difficult to transport.

452
00:25:44,220 --> 00:25:46,720
A tent like this would actually take about half an hour

453
00:25:46,820 --> 00:25:48,760
for a group of individuals that were trained

454
00:25:48,761 --> 00:25:50,360
to actually set up.

455
00:25:54,800 --> 00:25:58,020
Aluminum bracket supports developed in the 1970s

456
00:25:58,021 --> 00:25:59,960
improved the assembly process.

457
00:26:00,900 --> 00:26:03,740
The tents were also more compact and easier to transport.

458
00:26:04,880 --> 00:26:06,720
They allowed for larger shelters,

459
00:26:07,320 --> 00:26:09,020
but setup was still time-consuming.

460
00:26:09,580 --> 00:26:12,280
We had what was called medium-general purpose tent.

461
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:16,480
It would take 10 people two hours to set up that type of tent.

462
00:26:16,481 --> 00:26:20,980
Then, in 2009, a revolutionary development radically changed

463
00:26:21,080 --> 00:26:23,980
the military tent.

464
00:26:24,080 --> 00:26:27,120
It's called the Air Beam.

465
00:26:27,220 --> 00:26:30,620
This is the Tempur Air Beam supported tent.

466
00:26:30,720 --> 00:26:34,940
This particular tent actually enables up to 22 soldiers

467
00:26:35,040 --> 00:26:38,340
to fit in bunk beds and live comfortably.

468
00:26:38,440 --> 00:26:40,340
The Air Beam is a structural support

469
00:26:40,440 --> 00:26:42,840
made from tire-like material.

470
00:26:42,940 --> 00:26:46,480
It can inflate quickly to support virtually any size tent.

471
00:26:46,481 --> 00:26:50,980
It eliminated the need for labor-intensive aluminum

472
00:26:50,981 --> 00:26:52,480
brackets.

473
00:26:52,481 --> 00:26:55,620
The temperance themselves are individual air beams.

474
00:26:55,621 --> 00:26:59,220
These are a very rigid air beam.

475
00:26:59,221 --> 00:27:03,940
You can see that it's not going to go down.

476
00:27:03,941 --> 00:27:06,300
The air beams are remarkably sturdy.

477
00:27:06,301 --> 00:27:11,200
A single 20-foot beam can support a 10,000-pound load.

478
00:27:11,201 --> 00:27:13,940
The beams are constructed to function individually.

479
00:27:13,941 --> 00:27:16,200
This way, if one goes down, the whole tent

480
00:27:16,201 --> 00:27:18,960
won't go down with it.

481
00:27:18,961 --> 00:27:21,920
So if one gets punctured, you still have the rest of the tent.

482
00:27:21,921 --> 00:27:25,840
And you actually can bring in replacement air beams to outfit

483
00:27:25,841 --> 00:27:27,760
this tent if one were to get punctured.

484
00:27:31,720 --> 00:27:34,920
The manufacturing process of the air beam begins here,

485
00:27:34,921 --> 00:27:40,960
in Fairfield, Virginia, at HDT Engineered Technologies.

486
00:27:40,961 --> 00:27:42,760
The beams themselves are fabricated

487
00:27:42,761 --> 00:27:44,000
from high-strength fibers,

488
00:27:44,720 --> 00:27:46,380
braid it into a continuous length

489
00:27:46,381 --> 00:27:47,720
and coat it with a polymer,

490
00:27:47,980 --> 00:27:49,900
basically like a tire you fill and forget.

491
00:27:51,500 --> 00:27:54,380
Airbeam technology reduces the whole logistics burden

492
00:27:54,381 --> 00:27:55,420
on the guys out in the field.

493
00:27:57,500 --> 00:27:58,860
The biggest plus of all,

494
00:27:59,320 --> 00:28:01,080
the Airbeam tent sets up quickly.

495
00:28:01,540 --> 00:28:03,420
You basically plug it into an air compressor,

496
00:28:03,780 --> 00:28:04,860
and you've got a tent.

497
00:28:05,620 --> 00:28:08,780
With the Airbeam, you can do it in a matter of minutes.

498
00:28:08,781 --> 00:28:13,420
It's also exceptionally durable.

499
00:28:15,860 --> 00:28:19,340
The temporary air beam is actually tested against a military standard.

500
00:28:19,740 --> 00:28:23,200
It's tested against rain, wind, snow load.

501
00:28:23,460 --> 00:28:26,260
It's also tested against flame resistance.

502
00:28:28,520 --> 00:28:31,620
At NATO, they don't just test the air beams themselves.

503
00:28:32,280 --> 00:28:34,460
The entire tent is sent through the ringer.

504
00:28:35,200 --> 00:28:38,500
Today, they're testing the outer shell fabric on this two-man tent.

505
00:28:38,780 --> 00:28:41,780
Here at the Dorio Climate Chamber,

506
00:28:41,781 --> 00:28:44,780
a state-of-the-art testing environment.

507
00:28:44,781 --> 00:28:46,780
We can take the wind up to 40 miles per hour,

508
00:28:46,781 --> 00:28:49,780
rain up to 4 inches an hour.

509
00:28:49,781 --> 00:28:52,780
Not only can the chamber replicate a downpour,

510
00:28:52,781 --> 00:28:56,780
it can produce temperatures as high as 165 degrees

511
00:28:56,781 --> 00:29:00,780
and then drop them as low as negative 70 degrees Fahrenheit.

512
00:29:00,781 --> 00:29:02,780
Pretty much any weather condition on the planet,

513
00:29:02,781 --> 00:29:04,780
we can simulate it here.

514
00:29:04,781 --> 00:29:06,780
Cutting-edge gear is making soldiers safer

515
00:29:06,781 --> 00:29:08,020
and more battle ready.

516
00:29:13,420 --> 00:29:16,920
Today's military base is set up in a matter of hours.

517
00:29:17,020 --> 00:29:20,220
They're fortified with advanced perimeter protection,

518
00:29:20,320 --> 00:29:23,360
but they still face an inevitable problem.

519
00:29:23,460 --> 00:29:26,400
Eventually, supplies run low.

520
00:29:26,500 --> 00:29:30,100
Restocking camps is essential to sustaining a mission.

521
00:29:30,200 --> 00:29:34,040
A ground delivery is dangerous, as one in 46 convoys

522
00:29:34,140 --> 00:29:36,780
suffers a casualty.

523
00:29:36,781 --> 00:29:40,780
And some locations are simply inaccessible by road.

524
00:29:40,880 --> 00:29:43,180
This current war in Afghanistan,

525
00:29:43,280 --> 00:29:45,420
a lot of the locations where our guys are

526
00:29:45,520 --> 00:29:47,220
are in mountains where there are no roads.

527
00:29:47,320 --> 00:29:49,520
There are no other means resupplied.

528
00:29:49,620 --> 00:29:51,320
Airdrops are the preferred method

529
00:29:51,420 --> 00:29:54,840
for resupplying a forward operating base.

530
00:29:54,940 --> 00:29:57,400
Dropping supplies from 35,000 feet

531
00:29:57,500 --> 00:29:59,240
is safer than ground delivery.

532
00:30:02,140 --> 00:30:04,940
But the goods need to land close to their intended mark,

533
00:30:04,941 --> 00:30:07,780
but the soldiers are back to square one,

534
00:30:07,880 --> 00:30:09,780
running a convoy to fetch them.

535
00:30:12,780 --> 00:30:15,380
At the Proving Grounds in Yuma, Arizona,

536
00:30:15,480 --> 00:30:17,880
engineers are working on landing airdrops

537
00:30:17,980 --> 00:30:21,860
as close as possible to their intended targets,

538
00:30:21,960 --> 00:30:26,360
using a technology many of us know well, GPS.

539
00:30:26,460 --> 00:30:28,940
Each payload has a separate GPS system.

540
00:30:29,040 --> 00:30:31,600
After it fully deploys and is stable,

541
00:30:31,700 --> 00:30:33,640
it starts flying in a direction.

542
00:30:33,641 --> 00:30:36,640
it then turns towards the IP, the intended point of impact,

543
00:30:36,641 --> 00:30:38,140
and starts flying.

544
00:30:38,141 --> 00:30:41,640
Once launched, onboard GPS-enabled computers

545
00:30:41,641 --> 00:30:44,140
automatically steer the parachutes.

546
00:30:44,141 --> 00:30:46,140
GPS-guided system is sitting right here

547
00:30:46,141 --> 00:30:49,140
at the confluence of the suspension lines.

548
00:30:49,141 --> 00:30:52,640
The system makes adjustments based on preset coordinates,

549
00:30:52,641 --> 00:30:55,140
wind speed, and rate of descent.

550
00:30:55,141 --> 00:30:57,140
All right, it just made its turn.

551
00:30:57,141 --> 00:30:59,140
It's got 500 feet to go.

552
00:30:59,141 --> 00:31:02,140
It's trying to get back somewhere over here.

553
00:31:02,141 --> 00:31:04,740
In the past two years, researchers have reduced

554
00:31:04,840 --> 00:31:07,340
the distance from target by more than half,

555
00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:11,480
from 200 meters to consistently less than 100.

556
00:31:11,580 --> 00:31:14,180
I don't think we thought that we were going to get 100 meters

557
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:15,080
10 years ago.

558
00:31:23,100 --> 00:31:25,900
In Afghanistan over the past five years,

559
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:29,740
airdrop deliveries have increased over 17-fold.

560
00:31:29,741 --> 00:31:33,340
In 2010, the military dropped more than 60 million pounds

561
00:31:33,440 --> 00:31:34,180
of supplies.

562
00:31:39,180 --> 00:31:42,480
GPS technology is delivering on target.

563
00:31:42,580 --> 00:31:45,580
But to ensure the package lands safely,

564
00:31:45,680 --> 00:31:48,200
it's up to the parachute.

565
00:31:48,300 --> 00:31:50,720
At the Natick Research Lab in Massachusetts,

566
00:31:50,820 --> 00:31:53,100
refining the parachute is an ongoing assignment.

567
00:31:56,400 --> 00:31:59,300
This is an improved version of the low-cost, low-volved

568
00:31:59,301 --> 00:32:01,540
It's going to be lighter and cheaper

569
00:32:01,640 --> 00:32:03,800
and easier to handle for the soldiers.

570
00:32:05,940 --> 00:32:08,540
The parachutes are broken into two categories.

571
00:32:08,640 --> 00:32:11,740
The first is high velocity, designed to drop quickly

572
00:32:11,840 --> 00:32:13,240
at 85 feet per second.

573
00:32:13,340 --> 00:32:15,240
Building supplies and sturdier items

574
00:32:15,340 --> 00:32:16,960
can handle this kind of impact.

575
00:32:20,960 --> 00:32:23,860
The other type is low velocity, designed to drop

576
00:32:23,960 --> 00:32:26,660
at 25 feet per second.

577
00:32:26,661 --> 00:32:30,260
These chutes deliver food and fuel.

578
00:32:30,261 --> 00:32:33,260
They require a more cushioned landing

579
00:32:33,261 --> 00:32:36,300
and max out at 2,200-pound loads.

580
00:32:36,301 --> 00:32:39,700
They're used in 95% of resupply drops.

581
00:32:39,701 --> 00:32:43,140
Once the parachute is packed,

582
00:32:43,141 --> 00:32:46,220
the release is the easy part.

583
00:32:46,221 --> 00:32:48,940
This parachute will sit on top of here,

584
00:32:48,941 --> 00:32:52,620
and as this comes out of the airplane,

585
00:32:52,621 --> 00:32:54,980
these ropes will get pulled.

586
00:32:54,981 --> 00:32:57,140
it'll pull the parachute out of the bag.

587
00:33:03,620 --> 00:33:09,620
Every successful airdrop makes life safer for the soldier,

588
00:33:09,720 --> 00:33:12,340
keeping convoys off the road and reducing the distance

589
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:13,440
of package retrieval.

590
00:33:18,140 --> 00:33:21,080
Once the supplies land on base, the next trick

591
00:33:21,180 --> 00:33:23,980
is making them last longer.

592
00:33:23,981 --> 00:33:25,940
That's the goal of Catherine Hammack,

593
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:27,880
Assistant Secretary of the Army.

594
00:33:28,720 --> 00:33:31,160
If we're able to use less energy

595
00:33:31,161 --> 00:33:33,880
and use less water in our base camps

596
00:33:33,881 --> 00:33:35,960
as fewer convoys on the road,

597
00:33:36,300 --> 00:33:39,940
that's saving lives, and that's very important to us.

598
00:33:40,760 --> 00:33:43,820
At the Systems Integration Lab in Fort Devons, Massachusetts,

599
00:33:44,760 --> 00:33:48,040
scientists are working on ways to reduce fuel and water consumption.

600
00:33:48,500 --> 00:33:52,000
It accounts for 80% of the cargo on resupply missions.

601
00:33:53,980 --> 00:33:57,980
In Afghanistan, base camp generators run on fuel,

602
00:33:58,080 --> 00:34:02,980
but it costs the military an astonishing $400 per gallon,

603
00:34:03,080 --> 00:34:04,920
and it's dangerous to transport.

604
00:34:08,020 --> 00:34:11,040
To reduce fuel consumption, the army uses generators

605
00:34:11,140 --> 00:34:13,340
controlled by microgrid systems.

606
00:34:13,440 --> 00:34:15,640
They distribute the generator's power more efficiently

607
00:34:15,740 --> 00:34:17,940
throughout the camps.

608
00:34:18,040 --> 00:34:20,640
With this kind of microgrid system,

609
00:34:20,641 --> 00:34:24,220
system, we're able to link all those generators together.

610
00:34:24,221 --> 00:34:27,140
So you need fewer generators.

611
00:34:27,141 --> 00:34:30,520
On average, an entire 150-man base camp

612
00:34:30,521 --> 00:34:33,420
needs only six generators because of the microgrid

613
00:34:33,421 --> 00:34:35,560
computer technology.

614
00:34:35,561 --> 00:34:37,820
The generators are all networked together.

615
00:34:37,821 --> 00:34:40,100
So when the kitchen shuts down for the day,

616
00:34:40,101 --> 00:34:43,120
the generator powers down with it.

617
00:34:43,121 --> 00:34:45,300
And if just a small amount of power is needed,

618
00:34:45,301 --> 00:34:47,440
it's pulled more efficiently from other generators

619
00:34:47,441 --> 00:34:49,940
already in use.

620
00:34:49,941 --> 00:34:57,500
The return on investment's huge because we're seeing anywhere from a 20% to 50% fuel savings in these microgrids.

621
00:34:59,620 --> 00:35:04,880
Another concept currently in development that will help ease power consumption even further

622
00:35:04,881 --> 00:35:11,160
is a whole new bend on LED lighting, flexible electroluminescent lighting sheets.

623
00:35:11,820 --> 00:35:13,600
This is lighting of the future.

624
00:35:14,120 --> 00:35:18,020
If you can imagine, this is what our light is going to look like inside of a shelter.

625
00:35:18,021 --> 00:35:22,320
and this light can be Velcroed to the wall of the shelter

626
00:35:22,321 --> 00:35:24,880
very simply and very easily by the soldiers.

627
00:35:25,240 --> 00:35:29,660
What's great about this lighting is that we can unwrap it,

628
00:35:30,360 --> 00:35:32,620
we can put our hands on it, it's cool.

629
00:35:33,240 --> 00:35:35,660
This lighting is based on electroluminescence

630
00:35:35,661 --> 00:35:39,200
and through the chemical reaction, the electrons emit light.

631
00:35:39,700 --> 00:35:42,000
The Army hopes to adopt this lighting technology

632
00:35:42,001 --> 00:35:43,100
within the next decade.

633
00:35:43,460 --> 00:35:46,460
It's really an amazing setup that we have here

634
00:35:46,461 --> 00:35:50,800
to try some very new and somewhat radical kinds of things.

635
00:35:52,200 --> 00:35:53,800
Fueling a base is expensive,

636
00:35:54,400 --> 00:35:58,300
but in remote desert camps, water doesn't come cheap either.

637
00:35:59,340 --> 00:36:02,320
The cost of water right now overseas can range anywhere

638
00:36:02,321 --> 00:36:06,380
from $5 a gallon all the way up to $30 a gallon at remote sites.

639
00:36:07,040 --> 00:36:09,840
To help reduce both cost and resupply emissions,

640
00:36:10,280 --> 00:36:13,040
the Army adopted a shower recycling system.

641
00:36:13,940 --> 00:36:16,300
It's a water purification system.

642
00:36:16,460 --> 00:36:20,460
that we're using to filter our shower waste water

643
00:36:20,461 --> 00:36:24,460
and return it at about a 75% recovery rate

644
00:36:24,461 --> 00:36:25,560
back into the showers.

645
00:36:26,260 --> 00:36:29,300
We're looking at saving almost 9,000 gallons of water

646
00:36:29,301 --> 00:36:30,360
on a single day.

647
00:36:30,620 --> 00:36:32,920
So if you expand that out over the course of a year,

648
00:36:33,060 --> 00:36:35,300
we're looking at saving 3.3 million gallons.

649
00:36:35,940 --> 00:36:37,840
That translates to a savings

650
00:36:37,841 --> 00:36:41,160
of more than $115 million per year,

651
00:36:41,340 --> 00:36:44,580
plus less risk to soldiers making fewer resupply missions.

652
00:36:46,460 --> 00:36:53,460
Today, troops go through training like never before.

653
00:36:53,461 --> 00:36:58,460
Bases set up in a flash.

654
00:36:58,461 --> 00:37:01,460
And resupply missions are hitting the mark.

655
00:37:01,461 --> 00:37:06,460
But for a soldier to operate at peak performance,

656
00:37:06,461 --> 00:37:08,460
a very basic need must be met.

657
00:37:08,461 --> 00:37:10,460
The Army runs on his stomachs.

658
00:37:10,461 --> 00:37:14,460
A capability like this allows you to push that energy to soldiers

659
00:37:14,461 --> 00:37:16,560
so they can execute that mission.

660
00:37:17,420 --> 00:37:19,280
While stationed on a remote base,

661
00:37:19,560 --> 00:37:22,780
a hot meal is a key factor in keeping a soldier's morale up

662
00:37:22,781 --> 00:37:24,440
and performance at its best.

663
00:37:29,580 --> 00:37:32,140
The challenge for the military's force provider,

664
00:37:32,480 --> 00:37:33,560
or base in a box,

665
00:37:33,800 --> 00:37:36,040
is to ship a fully functioning kitchen

666
00:37:36,041 --> 00:37:38,180
capable of feeding the entire base.

667
00:37:39,140 --> 00:37:40,840
This whole kitchen,

668
00:37:41,060 --> 00:37:43,560
it folds up into a Tricon configuration

669
00:37:43,561 --> 00:37:47,360
and can be slung load even out to some of your remote areas.

670
00:37:48,400 --> 00:37:51,360
To help engineer a kitchen into one container,

671
00:37:52,200 --> 00:37:55,440
the Army turned to Tritec in Liberty, South Carolina.

672
00:38:00,340 --> 00:38:03,160
Here, they configure the entire kitchen.

673
00:38:03,820 --> 00:38:05,760
All the appliances are completely electric,

674
00:38:06,160 --> 00:38:07,080
which is much cleaner.

675
00:38:07,760 --> 00:38:09,460
It's a total electric system.

676
00:38:10,080 --> 00:38:12,600
It runs off a 200-amp electrical panel.

677
00:38:12,601 --> 00:38:14,600
There's no fuel, there's no odors.

678
00:38:14,601 --> 00:38:18,600
It is an improvement over the old combustion kitchens

679
00:38:18,601 --> 00:38:20,600
because you don't have a lot of that exhaust,

680
00:38:20,601 --> 00:38:22,600
and the food tastes better as well.

681
00:38:27,600 --> 00:38:30,600
When all the parts are finally cut, bent, and welded,

682
00:38:30,601 --> 00:38:33,600
Tritec meticulously packs it all up into the container.

683
00:38:36,600 --> 00:38:38,600
When loaded and ready to ship,

684
00:38:38,601 --> 00:38:41,600
the system weighs just over 7,600 pounds.

685
00:38:41,601 --> 00:38:47,200
This force provider kitchen can be set up and ready to cook

686
00:38:47,201 --> 00:38:49,600
hot meals in less than an hour.

687
00:38:49,601 --> 00:38:53,440
When the system is deployed, its dimensions are the standard

688
00:38:53,441 --> 00:38:56,640
eight by eight by six and a half.

689
00:38:56,641 --> 00:39:01,560
But like the eight-man bunking system, the sides slide out,

690
00:39:01,561 --> 00:39:05,600
giving it a 17-foot reach, enough room to cook for up to

691
00:39:05,601 --> 00:39:08,400
300 people three times a day.

692
00:39:08,401 --> 00:39:10,600
They actually looked at a flow pattern for this as well,

693
00:39:10,601 --> 00:39:13,600
as well. Soldiers can actually come right up to the window,

694
00:39:13,700 --> 00:39:16,600
and then they have a place to sit down and enjoy the food.

695
00:39:22,140 --> 00:39:24,140
Sometimes soldiers don't get to sit down

696
00:39:24,240 --> 00:39:25,440
for a hot-cooked meal.

697
00:39:25,540 --> 00:39:28,660
They have to grab their food and go.

698
00:39:28,760 --> 00:39:33,860
So troops depend on Meals Ready to Eat, or MREs.

699
00:39:33,960 --> 00:39:35,860
At the Fort Devins Research Lab,

700
00:39:35,960 --> 00:39:38,160
they work on perfecting the food.

701
00:39:38,161 --> 00:39:40,320
Well, right here I have a MRE,

702
00:39:40,420 --> 00:39:42,260
and basically this will provide that warfighter

703
00:39:42,360 --> 00:39:44,460
with about 1,300 calories per meal

704
00:39:44,560 --> 00:39:46,560
and all the proper percentages of fat, proteins,

705
00:39:46,660 --> 00:39:48,900
carbohydrates, vitamins and minerals.

706
00:39:53,600 --> 00:39:56,320
Rations have been around since the Revolutionary War,

707
00:39:56,420 --> 00:39:59,520
when packs consisted of ingredients like beef,

708
00:39:59,620 --> 00:40:03,820
hard bread, and spruce beer.

709
00:40:03,920 --> 00:40:06,060
Since World War II, the Tootsie Roll

710
00:40:06,160 --> 00:40:08,160
has been a staple ingredient.

711
00:40:08,161 --> 00:40:12,160
But when the MREs were introduced in 1975,

712
00:40:12,161 --> 00:40:14,160
they left a little to be desired.

713
00:40:14,161 --> 00:40:17,160
Since then, they've stepped it up in taste.

714
00:40:17,161 --> 00:40:21,160
I think anybody that's been in the service for any period of time

715
00:40:21,161 --> 00:40:25,160
would say that the rations have improved from day one till now,

716
00:40:25,161 --> 00:40:27,160
and that's really our mission.

717
00:40:27,161 --> 00:40:29,160
Not only does the food have to be tasty,

718
00:40:29,161 --> 00:40:31,160
it needs staying power.

719
00:40:31,161 --> 00:40:34,160
The requirement for the meal ready to eat is a three-year shelf life

720
00:40:34,161 --> 00:40:36,160
when stored at 80 degrees Fahrenheit or below.

721
00:40:36,161 --> 00:40:40,160
The meals can be heated, too, without a stove.

722
00:40:40,161 --> 00:40:43,160
Inside this package here, you have a pad that contains

723
00:40:43,161 --> 00:40:45,160
a magnesium and iron compound.

724
00:40:45,161 --> 00:40:48,160
So you add a tiny bit of water, the water reacts with the magnesium

725
00:40:48,161 --> 00:40:51,160
and creates what we call an exothermic chemical reaction,

726
00:40:51,161 --> 00:40:54,160
and it heats up that ration component in about 10 minutes.

727
00:40:54,161 --> 00:40:57,160
There are 24 different rotating meals,

728
00:40:57,161 --> 00:40:59,160
with a few more planned for next year.

729
00:40:59,161 --> 00:41:04,160
Mexican chicken stew, which will be added to the MRE procurement for 2012,

730
00:41:04,161 --> 00:41:07,160
and potatoes al gratin, which will also be added.

731
00:41:07,161 --> 00:41:10,160
They've gone through sensory analysis here in our facility,

732
00:41:10,161 --> 00:41:14,160
as well as field testing out on the battlefield with warfighters.

733
00:41:14,161 --> 00:41:17,160
100% warfighter tested, 100% warfighter approved.

734
00:41:21,160 --> 00:41:26,160
For the military, keeping our warfighters safe is job number one.

735
00:41:26,161 --> 00:41:29,160
Whether it's fire-resistant uniforms,

736
00:41:29,161 --> 00:41:33,160
reinventing the tried-and-true tent,

737
00:41:33,161 --> 00:41:37,200
or live action training with Hollywood special effects.

738
00:41:39,360 --> 00:41:43,080
Years of research have gone into making soldiers battle-ready

739
00:41:43,081 --> 00:41:45,700
and equipped with the latest technology,

740
00:41:46,560 --> 00:41:52,120
all in an effort to put troops in a better position

741
00:41:52,121 --> 00:41:56,020
to carry out their missions and return home safely.

